$internal
$flags trusted

subroutine login(ok, mode)

$catalogue $LOGIN

$include revstamp.h

$include syscom.h
$include header.h
$include parser.h
$include int$keys.h
$include debug.h

$include err.h
$include keys.h
$include keyin.h
$include keycode.h
* 20240219 mab user must be member of sdusers  
* 14 Aug 26 Windows port - is_grp_member is back.  The sdusers gate and the
*   per-account group test below both call it.  See PROJECT_STATUS.md 5.6.
deffun is_grp_member(user,group) calling '!is_grp_member'
* 29 Aug 26 Windows port - PRE_RELEASE_FIXES 56.  THE SECOND CALLER OF
*   !elevate, and until today there was deliberately only one.  ELEVATE's own
*   header says "the only caller of START and STOP is CPROC's LOGTO handling";
*   that sentence is amended there rather than left to be discovered here.
*   The owner's rule it rests on - "there should never be an elevation from
*   within a normal session" - is untouched: this is not within a session, it
*   is what decides which account the session begins in.
deffun elevate(action,arg) calling '!elevate'

   ok = @false
   admin.login = @false

* 16 Aug 26 Windows port - PROJECT_STATUS.md 7 step 4.  Every refusal below
*   leaves through terminate.connection, so the audit record is written there
*   once rather than at each goto, and this says why.  IT IS SET HERE SO THAT A
*   REFUSAL ADDED LATER AND NOT GIVEN A REASON STILL PRODUCES A RECORD - a
*   vague line in the trail is recoverable, a missing one is not.
   audit.reason = 'unspecified'
   audit.account = ''

* 22 Aug 26 Windows port - PROJECT_STATUS.md 7 step 9.  ASSIGNED HERE, not at
*   the gate, for the reason the block below gives: it is read by the audit
*   line at the end of this subroutine, which is reached on paths that never
*   run the gate at all, and BCOMP's "is not assigned a value" fails the whole
*   bootstrap.  '' is also the value that means "no command was given", which
*   is the interactive session and the common case.
   batch.command = ''

* 17 Aug 26 Windows port - PROJECT_STATUS.md section 8.  Set here, at the top,
*   rather than left to the three call sites that do set them, because BCOMP's
*   "is not assigned a value" is what bootstrap.py line 229 fails the whole
*   bootstrap on, and both are read in update.voc - textually far above the
*   get.acc.tier that assigns them.  '' is also the value that means "full VOC",
*   so if a call site added later forgets, the behaviour is the pre-tier one
*   rather than a silently reduced account.
   update.voc.tier = ''
   tier.acc.name = ''

* 21 Aug 26 Windows port - and cred.ok for the same reason and in the same
*   place: require.credential assigns it, and it is READ at the call site,
*   which BCOMP sees textually above the subroutine.  @true is also the value
*   that means "nothing to do", so a path added later that skips the test lets
*   the login through rather than refusing it silently.
   cred.ok = @true

   is.phantom = kernel(K$IS.PHANTOM, 0)

   * If the terminal type has not yet been set, work out what it is

   if mode = 0 then    ;* Interactive session startup
      s = system(7)
      if s = '' then
            * This user has come in from a command prompt of some sort.
            s = env('TERM')
* 19 Aug 26 Windows port - 'windows' rather than 'vt100'.  vt100 binds the
*   arrow keys to ESC O A/B/C/D, which a terminal sends only in application
*   cursor mode, and nothing in SD ever sends smkx to enter it - so under
*   vt100 no arrow key can arrive at all.  Its kbs is ^H for the same kind of
*   reason.  PROJECT_STATUS.md 5.18.
*   THE VOC login PARAGRAPH SETS THE TYPE AGAIN, unconditionally, but it runs
*   AFTER this subroutine returns (CPROC:411 against CPROC:324), so it decides
*   nothing that happens in here - see the next comment.  Both must still name
*   the same type.
            if s = '' then s = 'windows'
      end

* 22 Aug 26 Windows port - THE BATCH GATE, PROJECT_STATUS.md 7 step 9.  A
*   command given on the command line is now admitted by an ELEVATED session as
*   before, or by the invoking account's list in SDSYS batch.jobs, and refused
*   otherwise.  sd.c used to refuse every unelevated command line, which
*   refused the scheduled job this exists for.  See batch.permitted.
* 22 Aug 26 Windows port - ASK FOR THE TYPE, THEN CHECK IT LOADED.  UNTIL ONE
*   LOADS THERE ARE NO CAPABILITIES AT ALL, not just no cub1: tsettermtype()
*   calls free_terminfo() only AFTER the open succeeds (sdtermlb.c:167-173), so
*   a name with no entry leaves tinfo NULL on the FIRST call and sdtgetstr()
*   returns "" for every id (sdtermlb.c:395).
*
*   THE NAME COMES FROM THE ENVIRONMENT AND NEED NOT HAVE AN ENTRY: here
*   getenv("TERM") answers 'xterm-256color' even when Windows set no TERM at
*   all - the MSYS2 runtime supplies it - and terminfo/x holds only 'xterm'.
*   That is the whole of the 22 Aug backspace fault: @(IT$CUB) and
*   terminfo('kbs') were empty at the installer's password prompt because no
*   type had ever loaded.  PROJECT_STATUS.md 5.18.
*
*   IT CANNOT BE LEFT TO THE PARAGRAPH.  The clear screen and
*   require.credential's password prompt are both in this subroutine, so they
*   are always in the unrepaired state - which is why every measurement taken
*   at the ':' prompt said the capability was fine.
*
*   kernel(K$TERM.TYPE) RETURNS THE TYPE IN FORCE AFTER THE ATTEMPT and leaves
*   it unchanged on failure (op_kernel.c:226-232), so a mismatch is the
*   failure.  downcase first: settermtype() lowercases the name it stores, so
*   an upper-case TERM would not compare equal to itself.  TERM:245-246 is the
*   same test on the same two calls.
      s = downcase(s)
      if (kernel(K$TERM.TYPE, s) # s) and (s # 'windows') then
         s = 'windows'
         void kernel(K$TERM.TYPE, s)
      end

      * Set terminal depth and width

      s = env('LINES');
      if not(s matches '1N0N') then s = terminfo('lines')
      if s <= 0 then s = DEFAULT.DEPTH
      setpu PU$LENGTH, -1, max(s, MIN.DEPTH)

      s = env('COLUMNS');
      if not(s matches '1N0N') then s = terminfo('cols')
      if s <= 0 then s = DEFAULT.WIDTH
      setpu PU$WIDTH, -1, max(s, MIN.WIDTH)

      * Clear screen except for phantoms and single command execution

      if not(is.phantom) and system(1026) = '' then
         if not(bitand(kernel(K$COMMAND.OPTIONS, 0), CMD.QUIET)) then
            display @(-1) :
         end

         if system(7) = 'sdterm' then
            if getpu(PU$WIDTH, -1) < 256 and getpu(PU$LENGTH, -1) < 256 then
               display @(-256, getpu(PU$WIDTH, -1) + 256 * getpu(PU$LENGTH, -1)) :
            end
         end
      end
   end

   if mode = 2 or mode = 3 then     ;* Just doing a VOC upgrade or install
      if mode = 2 then              ;* VOC update
* 17 Aug 26 Windows port - this is UPDATE.ACCOUNT (CPROC internal verb 15), which
*   is ungated, so the account's own tier decides what it may copy back in.
*   PROJECT_STATUS.md section 8.
         tier.acc.name = @who
         gosub get.acc.tier
         update.voc.f = voc
         gosub update.voc
         close update.voc.f
      end

      if @who = 'SDSYS' and kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR,-1) then
         loop
            display sysmsg(5003) :  ;* Update VOC in all registered accounts (Y/N)?
            input yn
            yn = upcase(yn)
         until yn = 'Y' or yn = 'N'
         repeat

         if yn = 'Y' then
            voc.paths = @path
            openpath @sdsys:@ds:'accounts' to acc.f then
               select acc.f to 12
               loop
                  readnext acc.name from 12 else exit
                  read acc.rec from acc.f, acc.name then
                     acc.path = parse.pathname.tokens(acc.rec<ACC$PATH>)

                     locate acc.path in voc.paths<1> setting s else
                        s = acc.path:@ds:'voc'
                        openpath s to update.voc.f then
                           display sysmsg(5004, s) ;* Updating xx
* 17 Aug 26 Windows port - the register record is already read, so the tier comes
*   straight off it rather than through get.acc.tier - which would reopen
*   ACCOUNTS underneath this very walk.  PROJECT_STATUS.md section 8.
* 27 Aug 26 Windows port - AND A SUSPENDED ACCOUNT IS UPDATED AT THE TIER IT
*   HELD, not at SUSPENDED.  This walk visits every account in the register,
*   suspended ones included, and update.voc reads anything that is not blank,
*   PROGRAMMER or ADMINISTRATOR as "apply the standard omit list".  Without
*   this line a release update would quietly strip a suspended administrator's
*   VOC to a standard one, and lifting the suspension would not put it back:
*   the account would then be an administrator holding a standard VOC, which
*   is exactly the failure the tier field was added to stop.
                           update.voc.tier = acc.rec<ACC$TIER>
                           if upcase(update.voc.tier) = 'SUSPENDED' then
                              update.voc.tier = acc.rec<ACC$PRIOR.TIER>
                           end
                           gosub update.voc
                           close update.voc.f
                           voc.paths<-1> = acc.path
                        end
                     end
                  end
               repeat
               close acc.f
            end else
               display sysmsg(2200) ;* Cannot open accounts register
            end
         end
      end 

      ok = @true
      return
   end



   if not(bitand(kernel(K$COMMAND.OPTIONS, 0), CMD.QUIET)) then
* 20 Aug 26 Windows port - the dead pre-fork banner that sat here is gone.
*   It was commented out, so nothing ever printed it, but it named the
*   wrong product and was one character away from being visible at every
*   login.  It was the last such string in the tree.  The live banner is
*   three lines down; sdsys/changelog records the rebranding itself.
* rev 0.9.1 back to single rev track 
      * Modified by Composer AI - 2026/06/10.
      * Close version banner string; unclosed quote caused "Unrecognised statement".
      * display "SD, the Multivalue String Database, version " : SD.REV.STAMP : "(AI modified)
      display "SD Core for Windows, the Essential Multivalue String Database, version " : SD.REV.STAMP
      * --------------------
      display "(c) 2000-2026 by Ladybridge Systems and Other Contributors"
      display
      * following must be left to comply with clause 1 of the gpl licence
      display "This program is free software and is supplied with ABSOLUTELY NO WARRANTY."
      display "You are welcome to modify or redistribute this software subject to certain"
      display "conditions.  For details type CONFIG GPL, for contributors type CONFIG CONTRIB."
      display
   end

   * Open the ACCOUNTS register

   openpath @sdsys:@ds:'accounts' to acc.f else
      display sysmsg(2200) ;* Cannot open accounts register
      goto terminate.connection
   end

* 20240219 mab always run in secure mode, no changes allowed to K$SECURE flag!! * 
*               we also no longer allow telnet connections via the internal
*               telnet server, nor are we supporting serial connections
*               SO we should never be true at the follow test.
*               BUT just to be safe, terminate if we get here as Network/serial connection

  if system(42) or system(1027) then   ;* Network/serial connection
    logmsg sysmsg(5063, 'security test')  ;* Failed login authentication from %1
    goto terminate.connection
  end else
* 14 Aug 26 Windows port - the sdusers gate is back, and with it the operating
*   system identity it tests.  The operating system has already authenticated
*   this user, so SD asks it who they are and never asks for a password.
*   See PROJECT_STATUS.md section 5.6.
     * This user has come in from a command prompt.
     lgn.id = @logname

* 14 Aug 26 Windows port - INTERNAL MODE IS EXEMPT FROM THE sdusers GATE.
*   Owner's decision, 14 Aug 2026, and the reason is the bootstrap: it runs
*   "sd -internal SECOND.COMPILE", which logs in, and on a clean BUILD machine
*   sdusers DOES NOT EXIST - only the installer creates it.  Testing it here
*   made building the product depend on a group that only installing it
*   produces, which is circular and would have refused the bootstrap at the
*   door on any machine that had never had SD on it.
*
*   IT OPENS NO HOLE.  "sd -INTERNAL" names SDSYS for itself in sd.c, and SDSYS
*   now requires an elevated session, so an internal caller has already passed
*   a strictly harder test than sdusers membership before reaching this line.
*   See PROJECT_STATUS.md section 5.6.

* 29 Aug 26 Windows port - THE ADMINISTRATOR EXEMPTION THAT STOOD HERE IS GONE,
*   AND THE GATE IS NOW UNIFORM ACROSS ALL THREE TIERS.  PRE_RELEASE_FIXES 56
*   clause 2, reversed by the owner the same day it was written.
*
*   IT WAS ADDED BY 56 AND ITS REASON WAS 56.  The exemption existed because
*   the model of that morning gave an administrator NO ACCOUNT OF THEIR OWN, so
*   nothing ever put them in sdusers and this gate - which runs before the
*   account is chosen below - would have refused them at the door.  The owner
*   then restored the personal account: "that is precisely why administrators
*   also had a personal account.  They got SDSYS in one of two ways, by
*   starting SD in an elevated session or by logging to SD after logging into
*   their personal account."  An administrator has an account again, so they
*   are in sdusers again, and the exemption has nothing left to fix.
*
*   AND REMOVING IT IS WHAT MAKES HIS OTHER SENTENCE TRUE, so this is a
*   feature rather than a tidy: "if any are built outside of sd they do not
*   have access to sd until a matching standard or programmer account is
*   created in SD."  That property already held for STANDARD and PROGRAMMER -
*   this line is what enforces it - and the exemption was the single reason it
*   did not hold for administrators.  A Windows administrator created outside
*   SD now gets 5009 here, exactly as anybody else made outside SD does.
*
*   BE HONEST ABOUT WHAT IT BUYS.  A Windows administrator can add themselves
*   to sdusers, read the data tree, or run as SYSTEM, so this is AN EXPLICIT
*   ACT AND AN AUDIT TRAIL, NOT A BOUNDARY THAT HOLDS AGAINST THEM.  The owner
*   was shown that before ruling.
     if not(kernel(K$INTERNAL,-1)) then
        if not(is_grp_member(lgn.id,'sdusers')) then
           display sysmsg(5009) ;* This user is not registered for SD use
           audit.reason = 'not a member of sdusers'
           goto terminate.connection
        end
     end
  end

   s = pterm(PT$INVERT, @true)

   * Determine account
* 20240330 mab we no longer support forced accounts from $LOGINS
*   forced.account = lgn.rec<LGN$FORCE.ACCOUNT>
* 14 Aug 26 Windows port - the three cases are restored from this repository's
*   own pre-port source at commit f9edab0, which is the specification rather
*   than a model designed here.  Which account you land in follows from who the
*   operating system says you are and whether this session is elevated.  There
*   is nothing to type and nothing is asked for.
*   See PROJECT_STATUS.md section 5.6.
   initial.account = ''

   begin case
      case kernel(K$FORCED.ACCOUNT,0) # ''   ;* "sd -Aname", or -INTERNAL
         initial.account = upcase(kernel(K$FORCED.ACCOUNT,0))

         read acc.rec from acc.f, initial.account else
            display sysmsg(5018, initial.account) ;* Account %1 not in register
            audit.account = initial.account
            audit.reason = 'no such account in the register'
            goto terminate.connection
         end

* 20240330 mab must be privilaged to enter sdsys
* 14 Aug 26 Windows port - "privileged" means ELEVATED.  K$ADMINISTRATOR is
*   seeded from IsElevated() at process start, so being an administrator in the
*   SAM is not enough: you have to have used it.
* 15 Aug 26 Windows port - NOBODY LOGS IN TO AN ACCOUNT BUT THEIR OWN.
*
*   Owner's rule, 15 Aug 2026, and it covers administrators: "an administrator
*   has access to all accounts, ONCE THEY HAVE LOGGED INTO SD, NOT BEFORE".
*   So entry is always your own account, and LOGTO is what moves you - which is
*   where the grant is tested, and where an elevated session gets its access to
*   everything (CPROC, logto.authorised).  One door, one place the decision is
*   made, instead of two that had drifted apart.
*
*   WHAT THIS REPLACED, both of which let somebody stand in an account without
*   ever standing in their own: an elevated session skipped the ACC$GROUP test
*   here entirely, so "sd -ASOMEBODYELSE" admitted an administrator straight
*   into anybody's account; and "sd" elevated with no account named went to
*   SDSYS.  The second case is deleted outright, below.
*
*   -INTERNAL IS THE EXCEPTION AND HAS TO BE.  sd.c forces it to SDSYS, and it
*   is the install and bootstrap door: during a bootstrap no account exists for
*   anybody to land in and there is nothing to LOGTO from.  It is unpublished
*   and comes from a command line rather than a session.

         if kernel(K$INTERNAL,-1) then
            if initial.account = 'SDSYS' and not(kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR,-1)) then
               display sysmsg(10002) ;* SDSYS Account access is restricted to privileged users
               audit.reason = 'SDSYS requires an elevated session'
               goto terminate.connection
            end
         end else
            if initial.account # upcase(@logname) then
               display sysmsg(10051) ;* You can only log in to your own account
               audit.reason = 'not the caller own account'
               goto terminate.connection
            end
         end

* 20240219 mab sd command from console with no account specified, we default to
*              user's account
* 14 Aug 26 Windows port - and no account of that name is a refusal, not a
*   prompt.  No linked SD account means no login.
* 15 Aug 26 Windows port - AND IT IS NOW THE ONLY CASE THERE IS for a person.
*   The elevated-session case that stood here put an administrator into SDSYS
*   without their having logged in anywhere first; it is gone with the rule
*   above.  An administrator arrives in their own account like everybody else
*   and types LOGTO SDSYS.

* 29 Aug 26 Windows port - AN ADMINISTRATOR IS ELEVATED HERE AND LANDS IN
*   SDSYS.  Owner's model, 29 Aug 2026, PRE_RELEASE_FIXES 56: "Administrators
*   get an elevation prompt at login and are logged in as SDSYS.
*   Administrators do not have a regular account of their own."
*
*   ***THIS REVERSES THE 15 Aug 2026 RULE QUOTED DIRECTLY ABOVE***, which
*   deleted this very case - "'sd' elevated with no account named went to
*   SDSYS.  The second case is deleted outright."  He was shown that and took
*   it knowingly.  The 15 Aug reasoning was "an administrator has access to
*   all accounts, once they have logged into SD, not before"; the new model
*   answers it differently - an administrator's LOGIN *is* the elevation, so
*   there is no "before" left to protect.
*
* 29 Aug 26 Windows port - AND THE TEST IS BACK TO "IS THIS SESSION ELEVATED".
*   PRE_RELEASE_FIXES 56 clause 2, reversed by the owner the same day.  For
*   one afternoon this case tested K$OS.ADMINISTRATOR - "is this person an
*   administrator at all" - and asked UAC on their behalf, because the model
*   of that morning left them nowhere else to land.  The personal account is
*   restored, so there are TWO EXPLICIT ROUTES INTO SDSYS AND NOT ONE
*   AUTOMATIC ONE, in his words: "they got SDSYS in one of two ways, by
*   starting SD in an elevated session or by logging to SD after logging into
*   their personal account."
*
*   SO AN UNELEVATED ADMINISTRATOR FALLS THROUGH TO "case 1" AND LANDS IN
*   THEIR OWN ACCOUNT, and reaches SDSYS from there with LOGTO SDSYS, which
*   asks UAC in CPROC.  Elevating the terminal first is the other route.
*
*   ***K$ADMINISTRATOR HERE IS THE PROCESS-START SEED AND THAT IS THE WHOLE
*   MECHANISM.***  kernel.c:240 sets USR_ADMIN from IsElevated() && not
*   CN_SOCKET, and NOTHING HAS TOUCHED IT YET at this point: the only writers
*   in the BASIC layer are :615 below and CPROC:2769/:2781, and both CPROC
*   sites are in the LOGTO path, which cannot run before LOGIN.  So reading it
*   here asks "did this session START elevated", which is a question no other
*   key answers.
*
*   MEASURED 29 Aug 2026 RATHER THAN ASSUMED, with gplbld/probe-osadmin.ps1
*   run twice from the same account.  The two keys differ in exactly the place
*   this case needs them to:
*
*                          unelevated   elevated
*     IsAdmin()               TRUE        TRUE     -> K$OS.ADMINISTRATOR
*     IsElevated()            FALSE       TRUE     -> K$ADMINISTRATOR seed
*
*   ***SO K$OS.ADMINISTRATOR COULD NOT CARRY THIS ON ITS OWN*** - it is TRUE
*   for an unelevated administrator, which is the case that must NOT come
*   here.  Do not "simplify" this back to one key.
*
*   BOTH ARE TESTED, AND THE SECOND IS BELT TO THE FIRST'S BRACES.  Elevation
*   implies administrator in every ordinary case, so K$OS.ADMINISTRATOR adds
*   nothing on the happy path; it closes the case where a token carries
*   BUILTIN\Administrators for an account that is not a member of it, where
*   the seed would say "elevated" of somebody Windows does not call an
*   administrator.  It costs one kernel call at login.
*
*   ADMINISTRATORS KEEP ssh NOW, WHICH THE MORNING'S MODEL HAD TAKEN.  An ssh
*   session is never elevated - UAC has no desktop there - so it falls through
*   to case 1 and lands in the administrator's personal account, like anybody
*   else.  It is the account's rights that apply, not SDSYS's, which is the
*   whole point of clause 3.
*
*   THE REGISTER IS READ BEFORE UAC IS ASKED.  A broken install should not
*   draw a consent dialog to then refuse the login anyway.
*
*   elevate('START') IS KEPT EVEN THOUGH THIS SESSION IS ALREADY ELEVATED, and
*   it prompts for nothing: sd-elevate.ps1:119 short-circuits with exit 0 when
*   the process token is already an administrator's.  It stays because it is
*   what registers the session with the per-user helper, and because a failure
*   here is the honest way to refuse an elevation that cannot be served.
*
*   NOTHING IS LEAKED IF A LATER STEP FAILS.  The helper carries -OwnerPid and
*   exits when this session's process goes, waking periodically while idle, so
*   a refusal further down releases it without CPROC's logto.privilege.undo
*   dance.  Checked in sd-elevate-helper.ps1 rather than assumed.
      case kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR, -1) and kernel(K$OS.ADMINISTRATOR, 0)
         initial.account = 'SDSYS'

         read acc.rec from acc.f, initial.account else
            display sysmsg(5018, initial.account) ;* Account %1 not in register
            audit.account = initial.account
            audit.reason = 'SDSYS is not in the register'
            goto terminate.connection
         end

         if elevate('START', '') # 0 then
            display sysmsg(10002) ;* SDSYS Account access is restricted to privileged users
            audit.account = initial.account
            audit.reason = 'elevation refused or unavailable'
            goto terminate.connection
         end

         void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'ELEVATION GRANTED account=SDSYS')
         admin.login = @true

      case 1
         initial.account = upcase(@logname)

         read acc.rec from acc.f, initial.account else
            display sysmsg(5018, initial.account) ;* Account %1 not in register
            audit.account = initial.account
            audit.reason = 'no such account in the register'
            goto terminate.connection
         end
   end case

   audit.account = initial.account

* 27 Aug 26 Windows port - A SUSPENDED ACCOUNT IS NOT ENTERED.  Owner, 27 Aug
*   2026: a fourth tier "so that an account can be temporarily denied access".
*   MODIFY.ACCOUNT writes SUSPENDED to ACC$TIER and this is the door it shuts.
*
*   AFTER THE CASE STATEMENT, NOT INSIDE IT, so it covers both arms with one
*   test.  Both have acc.rec read by the time they leave, and both reach here;
*   putting it in each arm would be two chances to add a third arm later and
*   forget one.
*
*   NOTHING IS WITHDRAWN ON WINDOWS, so this test is the whole of the
*   suspension for this route.  Owner's ruling: the group memberships are left
*   exactly as they are, which is what makes lifting a suspension free - there
*   is no prior state to record and restore.  The cost, stated rather than
*   hidden: the ssh connection is still accepted and sd still starts, and the
*   person is refused here rather than by sshd.
*
*   AND IT IS UNCONDITIONAL, elevation included.  "sd -internal" is forced to
*   SDSYS by sd.c and MODIFY.ACCOUNT refuses to touch SDSYS at all (2202), so
*   there is no account this can lock a bootstrap out of.
   if upcase(trim(acc.rec<ACC$TIER>, ' ', 'B')) = 'SUSPENDED' then
      display sysmsg(10107, initial.account) ;* Account %1 is suspended
      audit.reason = 'the account is suspended'
      goto terminate.connection
   end

   acc.path = parse.pathname.tokens(acc.rec<ACC$PATH>)

   if not(ospath(acc.path, OS$CD)) then
      display sysmsg(5019, acc.path) ;* Unable to change to directory xx
      goto terminate.connection
   end


   * Set @PATH

   account.path = ospath("", OS$CWD)

   initial.account.path = account.path
   if initial.account = '' then
      * Try to work out account name by checking to see if it is in the
      * ACCOUNTS register.

      select acc.f to 12
      loop
         readnext acc.id from 12 else exit
         read acc.rec from acc.f, acc.id then
            acc.path = parse.pathname.tokens(acc.rec<ACC$PATH>)
            if account.path = acc.path then
               initial.account = acc.id
               exit
            end
         end
      repeat
      clearselect 12

      if initial.account = '' then
         * Just use final part of pathname
         initial.account = upcase(account.path[index(account.path, @ds, count(account.path, @ds)) + 1, 99999])
      end
   end

   who = initial.account

* 29 Aug 26 Windows port - PRE_RELEASE_FIXES 56.  AFTER THE MOVE, NOT INSIDE
*   THE CASE, and for the reason CPROC:2723 gives at the same moment: the
*   directory change above can still fail, and the flag must not outlive a
*   move that did not happen.  The elevation is obtained early because the
*   case has to know whether it succeeded; the RIGHTS are granted here.
   if admin.login then
      void kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR, 1)
   end
   close acc.f

   * Set @TTY

   begin case
      case is.phantom          ; tty = 'phantom'
      case system(42) # ''     ; tty = 'telnet'
      case system(1027) # ''   ; tty = 'port'
      case 1                   ; tty = kernel(K$TTY, 0)
   end case

   * Open the VOC file

   openpath "voc" to voc on error
      display 'Error ' : status() : ' opening VOC file. Check access rights.'
      goto terminate.connection
   end else
   * 20240219 mab as part of the security changes, do not allow auto creation of account !
   *   gosub create.account
       display sysmsg(5163)    ;* Directory is not a  sd account
       goto terminate.connection 
   end

   if catalogued('CCALL.INIT') then call ccall.init


   * Open IPC file and delete any items for old versions of this process id

   openpath @sdsys:@ds:'$ipc' to ipc.f then
      recordlocku ipc.f,'M':@userno     ;* Message queue
      delete ipc.f, 'M':@userno

      recordlocku ipc.f,'P':@userno     ;* Phantom register
      delete ipc.f, 'P':@userno

      void fcontrol(ipc.f, FC$NON.TXN, 0) ;* Set non-transactional
   end

   if not(is.phantom) and not(fileinfo(voc, FL$READONLY)) then
      read voc.rec from voc, "$RELEASE" then
         * ----- Field 2 - Release level
         s = matchfield(voc.rec<2>, "0X0A", 1)
         if s # SD.REV.STAMP then
            display sysmsg(5025, s) ;* Your VOC is at release level xx
            loop
               display char(7) :
               display sysmsg(5026) :  ;* Update VOC to new release?
               input s

               s = upcase(s[1,1])
               begin case
                  case s = "N"
                     exit
                  case s = "Y"
* 17 Aug 26 Windows port - THIS IS THE ONE THAT NEEDED NO VERB.  Any user gets
*   this prompt whenever their VOC's release level differs from SD.REV.STAMP, and
*   before the tier was recorded, answering Y restored every capability
*   CREATE.ACCOUNT had withheld.  PROJECT_STATUS.md section 8.
                     tier.acc.name = @who
                     gosub get.acc.tier
                     update.voc.f = voc
                     gosub update.voc
                     close update.voc.f
                     exit
               end case
               display sysmsg(5027) ;* Please answer Y or N"
            repeat
         end

         * ----- Field 3 - Command processing modes
         s = voc.rec<3>
         cursor.at.end = (index(s, 'E', 1) # 0)
         command.overlay = (index(s, 'O', 1) # 0)
         show.stack = (index(s, 'S', 1) # 0)
         clear.on.data = not(index(s, 'X', 1)) and not(cursor.at.end)
      end else
         display sysmsg(5028) ;* $RELEASE VOC record not found
         goto terminate.connection
      end

      * Cross-check revisions

      if compare(system(1012), SD.REV.STAMP) then
         display sysmsg(5029) ;* Revision level cross-check error
         if not(kernel(K$INTERNAL,-1)) then goto terminate.connection
      end

      * Look for a saved command stack

* 19 Aug 26 Windows port - both spellings, and this one has no lock to release
*   so it needs no cs.id.  A record read matches the id exactly - there is no
*   fold on it - and an account created before the rename still holds the upper
*   case one.  See CPROC's copy of this for the full reasoning.
*   PROJECT_STATUS.md 5.12 (b).
      read voc.rec from voc,'$command.stack' else
         read voc.rec from voc,'$COMMAND.STACK' else
            voc.rec = ''
         end
      end
      if upcase(voc.rec[1,1]) = "X" THEN

        openpath initial.account.path:@ds:'stacks' to stk.f then
           read command.stack from stk.f, @logname else null
           close stk.f
        end

      end
   end

* 21 Aug 26 Windows port - AN ACCOUNT WITH NO PASSWORD SETS ONE HERE, BEFORE IT
*   REACHES THE PROMPT.  Owner's decision, 21 Aug 2026.  It was moved out of
*   phase 2 of the plan and into phase 3 deliberately: the flow it exists to
*   serve is an install that ends in an SD session and takes the installing
*   user's password, and putting a rule in front of every login while the flow
*   that needs it does not exist yet is a rule nobody can test.
*
*   IT IS SELF-LIMITING.  A password has been mandatory at creation since phase
*   2, so the only account that can reach this line without one is the ADOPTed
*   installing user's - ADOPT creates no Windows user and sets no password -
*   plus anything made before that rule existed.
*
*   ELEVATED SESSIONS ONLY, AND THAT IS THE ACL RATHER THAN A PREFERENCE.
*   secure-cred.ps1 locks $cred to SYSTEM and Administrators, and in stage 1 SD
*   runs as the invoking user (5.7), so an ordinary session can neither READ
*   the register to find out whether a credential is there nor WRITE one if it
*   is not.  Without this test the rule would read "no credential" for every
*   ordinary user on the machine and then be unable to do anything about it -
*   a refusal with no way back, for a console login that has never asked for a
*   password in the first place.  The account this exists for is an
*   administrator's by construction: ADOPT forces tier ADMINISTRATOR and the
*   installer is elevated.  When 5.7's service model lands and $cred is reached
*   through the server rather than the caller's token, this test is what comes
*   out.
*
*   -INTERNAL IS EXEMPT AND HAS TO BE.  bootstrap.py runs "sd -internal
*   SECOND.COMPILE" into an SDSYS that has no credential and with nothing to
*   answer a prompt, so the rule would refuse the build of the product itself.
*   Same exemption, same reason, as the sdusers gate above.
*
*   AND SO IS A SESSION WITH NOBODY AT A KEYBOARD, which is the K$TTY test and
*   is not a nicety.  "sd" reading a piped script is how every verifier in
*   gplbld drives this product - Invoke-SD pipes "LOGTO SDSYS", "TERM 200,9999"
*   and the rest into it - and a prompt in front of that would eat the script a
*   line at a time as password attempts and refuse the session.  The whole
*   suite would fail in a way that reads as a broken feature.  It is the honest
*   rule rather than a way round the tests - ask where a password can be typed
*   - and it closes nothing, because this asks for a password to be SET so the
*   account is reachable from elsewhere, not to let anybody in here.
*
*   K$TTY IS ttyname(fileno(stdin)) - kernel.c:250 - AND IT WAS MEASURED, not
*   assumed, on 21 Aug 2026 with a probe built by the MSYS2 gcc: piped stdin
*   gives isatty 0 and ttyname NULL, a real console window - which is exactly
*   what Inno's Exec with SW_SHOW hands sd.exe - gives isatty 1 and
*   "/dev/cons0".  An ssh session is a pty and answers the same way.
*
*   A PHANTOM IS EXEMPT because there is nobody to ask, and mode is tested
*   because mode 2 is UPDATE.ACCOUNT copying a VOC rather than a login.  AN API
*   SESSION CANNOT REACH THIS AT ALL: linuxio.c:112 gives it $APISRVR as its
*   command processor instead of $CPROC, and $CPROC is the only caller of this
*   routine at mode 0.
*
*   NEITHER CAN A GROUP ACCOUNT, and the reason is worth stating rather than
*   relying on.  Entry is always your own account, so reaching this line needs
*   initial.account = upcase(@logname), and a group account has no Windows user
*   of that name for anybody to log in as.
*
*   BEFORE THE AUDIT RECORD BELOW, so a session that ends here is written down
*   as a refusal rather than as a login that happened to stop.
*   24 Aug 26 Windows port - AND A COMMAND LINE IS EXEMPT.  Owner's ruling:
*   "sd <command>" is BATCH, so it is not interactive, so it must not ask.
*
*   THIS IS NOT THE SAME EXEMPTION AS K$TTY AND THAT IS THE POINT.  K$TTY asks
*   whether there is a terminal; this asks whether anybody is being invited to
*   sit at it.  "sd COUNT VOC" typed at a console HAS a tty - K$TTY answers
*   /dev/cons0 - and still must not stop to set a password: it is a one-shot
*   command that runs and exits, not a session somebody is entering.  A
*   scheduled job was already covered by K$TTY; this covers the console case
*   that was not, which is the one that HUNG for a day and was mis-diagnosed
*   three times (PROJECT_STATUS.md 7 step 9, and HISTORY's three entries).
*
*   batch.command IS HOISTED OUT OF THE BATCH GATE BELOW rather than calling
*   system(1026) twice.  Same guard, so it is set in exactly the cases it was
*   before and the audit record at the end of this routine is unchanged.
*
*   WHAT IT DOES NOT WEAKEN: an interactive "sd" with no command still asks,
*   which is the case the 21 Aug rule was written for - an administrator
*   signing in gets a password set so the account is reachable from the API
*   and from another machine.  Nothing here lets anybody IN; it asks for a
*   password to be SET.
   if mode = 0 and not(is.phantom) and not(kernel(K$INTERNAL,-1)) then
      batch.command = trim(system(1026))
      if kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR,-1) and kernel(K$TTY,0) # '' and batch.command = '' then
         gosub require.credential
         if not(cred.ok) then
            audit.reason = 'the account has no password and none was set'
            goto terminate.connection
         end
      end
   end

* 22 Aug 26 Windows port - THE BATCH GATE.  PROJECT_STATUS.md 7 step 9.
*
*   WHAT IT IS FOR.  A scheduled job signs in as a Windows batch account, and
*   if that account has an SD account it lands here with nothing asked - there
*   is no credential anywhere in this path and there must not be.  What was
*   missing is a limit on WHAT it may then run, and sd.c used to supply a
*   cruder one: an unelevated command line was refused outright, which refused
*   the batch account too.
*
*   ELEVATION STILL PASSES ON ITS OWN, which is step 7's shape and the owner's
*   decision of 22 Aug 2026.  An elevated session reaches this line and goes
*   straight through, so nothing an administrator does today changes.  Everyone
*   else needs the command on their account's list.
*
*   IT IS HERE, LAST, ON PURPOSE.  Everything above can still refuse, and a
*   refusal here leaves through terminate.connection like every other, so it is
*   written to the audit trail with a reason and without a second call.  CPROC
*   does not pick the command up until its single-command branch, which is
*   after this whole subroutine has returned - so nothing has run yet.
*
*   A PHANTOM IS EXEMPT because its command does not arrive this way at all -
*   it comes from the user table and CPROC handles it in a separate branch.  An
*   INTERNAL session is exempt because that is the install path, which sd.c
*   gates on its own.  Mode 0 for the same reason as the block above: mode 2 is
*   UPDATE.ACCOUNT copying a VOC, not a login.
*   24 Aug 26 - batch.command IS NOW SET BY THE BLOCK ABOVE, under the same
*   guard, because the password exemption added that day needs it too.  The
*   assignment moved; nothing else here changed.
   if mode = 0 and not(is.phantom) and not(kernel(K$INTERNAL,-1)) then
      if batch.command # '' and not(kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR,-1)) then
         gosub batch.permitted
         if not(batch.ok) then goto terminate.connection
      end
   end

* 16 Aug 26 Windows port - PROJECT_STATUS.md 7 step 4.  The single point at
*   which a login has succeeded, which is why the record is written here and
*   not at the account tests: everything above can still refuse.  The identity
*   is NOT passed - audit_message() stamps it from the user table, because
*   CPROC reassigns logname when it drops to sdsys (5.6).
*
*   22 Aug 26 - THE COMMAND IS NAMED IN THE RECORD when there is one, because
*   "LOGIN account=x" alone cannot distinguish a person signing in from a job
*   that ran something.  It is empty for every interactive session.
   if batch.command = '' then
      void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'LOGIN account=' : audit.account)
   end else
      void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'LOGIN account=' : audit.account : ' command=' : batch.command)
   end

   ok = @true
   return

terminate.connection:
* 16 Aug 26 Windows port - PROJECT_STATUS.md 7 step 4.  EVERY refused login
*   leaves through here, so one record covers all of them and a refusal added
*   later is recorded whether or not whoever adds it thinks about the trail.
   void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'LOGIN REFUSED account=' : audit.account : ' reason=' : audit.reason)

   display sysmsg(5024) ;* Connection terminated
   sleep 2
abort.login:
   return to abort.login

*
* ======================================================================
* 14 Aug 26 Windows port - AUTHENTICATE.ACCOUNT HAS BEEN DELETED, and the
* credential machinery it called has NOT.  Login takes no password: the
* operating system authenticated this user before SD started, and the two
* gates above are group membership and elevation.
*
* $CRED, !CRED_SET, !CRED_VERIFY and MODIFY.PASSWORD all remain, deliberately, on
* the repository owner's decision of 14 Aug 2026.  The API is a separate door
* and it does require an account password on top of the ssh tunnel, so the
* credential register changes owner rather than becoming dead code - it stops
* being the console's gate and becomes the API's.  Until APISRVR is brought
* under it (PROJECT_STATUS.md section 7 step 6) those four have no caller, and
* that is a recorded state rather than an oversight.
* See PROJECT_STATUS.md sections 5.6 and 8.
*
* 21 Aug 26 Windows port - AND THAT LAST SENTENCE IS NOW OUT OF DATE IN BOTH
* HALVES.  APISRVR came under $CRED on 19 Aug, and this file gained a caller of
* !CRED_SET below.  WHAT HAS NOT CHANGED IS THE FIRST PARAGRAPH: login still
* takes no password.  require.credential asks for one to be SET when the
* account has none; it never asks for one to be TYPED to get in, and the two
* are easy to confuse from a distance.
*
* ======================================================================
* REQUIRE.CREDENTIAL  -  the account must have a password.  Sets cred.ok.
*
* Reached only from an elevated, interactive, non-phantom, non-internal
* session; the call site carries the whole of why.
*
* IT DOES NOT ASK FOR A CURRENT PASSWORD, because there is not one - having
* none is the entire condition for being here.  That is the same branch
* SET_ACC_PASSWORD takes when its has.cred is false.
*
* THE WORDING IS DELIBERATELY MODIFY.PASSWORD'S.  That verb is where a password
* is set every other time, and somebody who meets both should not have to work
* out whether they are the same act.

require.credential:
   cred.ok = @true

*  A VERSION 1 RECORD READS AS "NO CREDENTIAL", exactly as it does in
*  SET_ACC_PASSWORD: it holds an Argon2 verifier and no StoredKey, cannot be
*  checked by !CRED_VERIFY and cannot be converted without the plaintext, which
*  was never stored.  So the first login after the upgrade replaces it.
   has.cred = @false
   openpath @sdsys:@ds:'$cred' to cred.f then
      read cred.rec from cred.f, initial.account then
         if cred.rec<CRED$STOREDKEY> # '' then has.cred = @true
      end
      close cred.f
   end else
*     FAIL OPEN, AND ONLY HERE.  An unreadable register means the install did
*     not finish or its ACL is wrong, and refusing every elevated login for
*     that takes away the one session that could put it right.  It grants
*     nothing the console does not already have - the console has never asked
*     for a password.  The API fails CLOSED on the same condition, in
*     !CRED_VERIFY, which is the door where it matters.
      display sysmsg(10088)  ;* Cannot open the credential register...
      return
   end

   if has.cred then return

   display sysmsg(10089, initial.account) ;* Account %1 needs a password...

*  22 Aug 26 Windows port - A BLANK LINE BETWEEN THE EXPLANATION AND THE PROMPT.
*  Owner, 22 Aug 2026, on seeing the paragraph land hard against "New password:".
*  It is here rather than as a trailing line break in the message because 10089
*  is a paragraph and this is spacing between two separate things - a message
*  that ends in blank lines invites the next editor to trim them as trailing
*  whitespace.
   display

* 26 Aug 26 Windows port - THREE ATTEMPTS, NOT AN UNBOUNDED LOOP.  Owner,
*   26 Aug 2026, after mistyping the password during an install and finding the
*   session had left with none set.
*
*   WHAT HE ACTUALLY HIT, BECAUSE THE LOOP ALREADY RE-PROMPTED AND WOULD HAVE
*   DONE SO FOR EVER.  After "The passwords do not match" it asked "New
*   password:" again - and 10089's paragraph, which explains that Enter on an
*   empty line means "no password for now", had scrolled away several lines
*   earlier.  Pressing Enter there is the documented way out and at that moment
*   it does not look like one.  So the count below is half the fix; SAYING
*   WHICH ATTEMPT THIS IS, in 10092, is the half that stops somebody reaching
*   for Enter to escape a loop they believe is endless.
*
*   AND BOUNDING IT RETIRES THE ORIGINAL REASON FOR THE EMPTY-LINE EXIT, which
*   the note below gives as "otherwise a session that cannot set a credential
*   would loop with no way to leave but killing the process".  That cannot
*   happen now.  The exit stays because 10089 offers it as a deliberate choice,
*   not because it is the only way out.
   cred.max.tries = 3
   cred.tries = 0

   loop
      cred.tries += 1
      display sysmsg(10090) :  ;* New password:
      input pw1 HIDDEN
      display

*     AN EMPTY PASSWORD IS THE WAY OUT, and it needs to be one: without it a
*     session that cannot set a credential - a wrong ACL, a full disk - would
*     loop with no way to leave but killing the process.
      if pw1 = '' then exit

      display sysmsg(10091) :  ;* Repeat new password:
      input pw2 HIDDEN
      display

*     WRITTEN AS if-match RATHER THAN if-mismatch-then-CONTINUE, deliberately.
*     CONTINUE inside a LOOP is demonstrated in update.voc below, but only as a
*     single-line "then continue" in the loop body itself; putting one inside a
*     block IF would be the first of its kind here, and this needs no new form.
      if pw1 = pw2 then
*        A COPY, NOT initial.account ITSELF.  BASIC passes by reference and
*        !CRED_SET assigns upcase(trim()) to its first argument, so handing it
*        the variable the rest of this routine and the audit record below are
*        keyed on would let a change there reach back into this one.
         cred.acc = initial.account
         cred.ok = @false
         call !CRED_SET(cred.acc, pw1, cred.ok)
         if cred.ok then
            display sysmsg(10093, initial.account) ;* Password set for account %1
            return
         end

         display sysmsg(10094, status()) ;* Unable to set the password, status %1
      end else
*        Attempt N of M, so the loop is visibly finite from inside it.
         display sysmsg(10092, cred.tries, cred.max.tries)

         if cred.tries >= cred.max.tries then
            cred.ok = @false
*           A DIFFERENT MESSAGE FROM THE EMPTY-LINE EXIT BELOW, because they
*           are different things: one is a choice, this is running out of
*           tries.  Both end the session, and 10101 says so and says that the
*           next login will ask again.
            display sysmsg(10101, cred.max.tries)
            return
         end
      end
   repeat

   cred.ok = @false
   display sysmsg(10095) ;* No password was set...
   return

*
* ======================================================================
* BATCH.PERMITTED  -  may this account run this command from the command line?
*
* PROJECT_STATUS.md 7 step 9.  Sets batch.ok, and on a refusal sets
* audit.reason and displays which of the three refusals it was.  It NEVER sets
* batch.ok true by falling through: every path that does not reach the last
* line leaves it false, which is the direction a permission list has to fail.
*
* THE LIST IS @SDSYS/batch.jobs, ONE RECORD PER ACCOUNT, one command name per
* field.  Section 8 designed it as an X-type record in SDSYS's VOC; it is a
* file instead, because SDSYS voc grants sdusers Modify - measured 22 Aug 2026
* by writing into it from an ordinary token - and a VOC record cannot carry an
* ACL of its own.  secure-osusers.ps1 locks this file read-only to sdusers, the
* same control and the same script as step 7's os.users.
*
* KEYED BY initial.account, WHICH IS ALSO THE ANSWER TO SECTION 8's "-A must
* match".  The forced account from "sd -Axxx" is what initial.account already
* holds by the time this runs, so consulting it cannot consult one account's
* list while running in another - there is no second name to disagree with.

batch.permitted:
   batch.ok = @false

*  ONE TOKEN, NO ARGUMENTS, AND THIS IS THE LINE DOING THE SECURITY WORK.
*  Section 8: "must be in the VOC" is worth nothing on its own, since every
*  verb is a VOC item - it is the absence of arguments that stops a listed
*  name being turned into something else.
   if index(batch.command, ' ', 1) then
      display sysmsg(10097)  ;* A command run this way takes no arguments
      audit.reason = 'command line carried arguments'
      return
   end

   openpath @sdsys:@ds:'batch.jobs' to batch.f else
      display sysmsg(10096, batch.command, initial.account)
      audit.reason = 'batch.jobs could not be opened'
      return
   end

   read batch.rec from batch.f, downcase(initial.account) else batch.rec = ''
   close batch.f

*  BOTH SHAPES ARE READ, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.  The dictionary describes
*  COMMAND as field 1 multivalued, which is what LIST needs and what the value
*  marks in "a]b" mean; somebody editing with ED will as naturally put one name
*  on each LINE.  Reading field marks and value marks alike lets the two agree
*  instead of one of them silently granting nothing.  It cannot admit anything
*  extra either way - the name still has to match exactly.
   batch.listed = @false
   batch.list = change(batch.rec, @fm, @vm)
   batch.n = dcount(batch.list, @vm)
   for batch.i = 1 to batch.n
      if downcase(trim(batch.list<1, batch.i>)) = downcase(batch.command) then
         batch.listed = @true
      end
   next batch.i

   if not(batch.listed) then
      display sysmsg(10096, batch.command, initial.account)
      audit.reason = 'command is not on the account list'
      return
   end

*  PA AND S ONLY.  Section 8's reason is that a mislisted name should fail when
*  somebody sets it up rather than at 3am - this cannot fully deliver that
*  until there is a verb to edit the list with (step 10), but it does refuse
*  with a message that names the type problem rather than "not permitted".
*
*  THE THREE-CASE FOLD, as CPROC does it at its own VOC read: as typed, then
*  lower, then upper.  5.12.
   read batch.voc.rec from voc, batch.command else
      read batch.voc.rec from voc, downcase(batch.command) else
         read batch.voc.rec from voc, upcase(batch.command) else
            display sysmsg(10098, batch.command)
            audit.reason = 'no VOC record for the command'
            return
         end
      end
   end

*  THE TYPE IS THE LEADING CHARACTERS OF FIELD 1 AND THE REST MAY BE PROSE -
*  "File for BASIC programs" is how CREATEA writes a file pointer, so a test
*  for equality with 'PA' would reject a perfectly good paragraph.  Compare the
*  leading characters, which is what SD itself dispatches on.
   batch.type = upcase(trim(batch.voc.rec<1>))
   if batch.type[1,2] # 'PA' and batch.type[1,1] # 'S' then
      display sysmsg(10098, batch.command)
      audit.reason = 'VOC type is not PA or S'
      return
   end

   batch.ok = @true
   return

*
* ======================================================================
* UPDATE.VOC  -  Copy NEWVOC to VOC

update.voc:
   yn = ''

   openpath @sdsys:@ds:"newvoc" to sdsys.file else
      display sysmsg(2210) ;* Cannot open SDSYS NEWVOC
      goto abort.login
   end

* 17 Aug 26 Windows port - THE TIER FILTER, PROJECT_STATUS.md section 8.  THIS
*   ROUTINE WAS THE HOLE IN THE VOC TIERS.  CREATEA gives a standard account
*   NEWVOC less the capabilities in TIER.OMIT.STANDARD; this routine then
*   re-copied the whole of NEWVOC and handed them straight back - the compiler,
*   the cataloguer, both editors, COPY and DELETE.CATALOG.
*
*   AND IT IS REACHED WITHOUT ANY PRIVILEGE.  Two ways: UPDATE.ACCOUNT, which is
*   CPROC internal verb 15 and tests nothing, and - worse, because it needs no
*   verb at all - the $RELEASE test below, which asks any user "Update VOC to new
*   release?" whenever the account's release level differs from SD.REV.STAMP.
*   One Y and the tier was gone.  So the tiers survived exactly until the next
*   release stamp, which is not a boundary anybody would have noticed moving.
*
*   THE TIER COMES FROM THE ACCOUNTS RECORD, field 5, written by CREATEA.  The
*   caller sets update.voc.tier, because the three call sites know the account by
*   two different routes: two of them are the current account (@who) and the
*   third is walking the whole register with the record already in hand.
*
*   BLANK MEANS THE FULL VOC - the pre-tier behaviour.  See SYSCOM/KEYS.H for
*   why that is the safe way round rather than reading blank as STANDARD.
   omit.rec = ''
   omit.tier = upcase(update.voc.tier)
   if omit.tier # '' and omit.tier # 'PROGRAMMER' and omit.tier # 'ADMINISTRATOR' then
      read omit.rec from sdsys.file, 'TIER.OMIT.STANDARD' else omit.rec = ''
   end
   omit.count = dcount(omit.rec, @fm)

   select sdsys.file to 11
   loop
      readnext id from 11 else exit

      copy.it = @true

*     The list records are never themselves VOC entries, whichever tier this is.
*     Before this they were copied in like any other record, so any account that
*     ran UPDATE.ACCOUNT acquired a TIER.OMIT.STANDARD entry in its VOC.  Inert -
*     their descriptions start with T, which is not a VOC type letter - but it
*     had no business being there.
* 18 Aug 26 Windows port - CASE INSENSITIVE, PROJECT_STATUS.md 5.12 (b).  id
*     comes from READNEXT over a DIRECTORY file, so it is the name as it stands
*     on disk.  A rename of the VOC ids would make these two comparisons miss
*     and put both list records into every account's VOC.
      if upcase(id) = 'TIER.OMIT.STANDARD' then copy.it = @false
      if upcase(id) = 'TIER.ADD.ADMINISTRATOR' then copy.it = @false

*     An explicit comparison rather than LOCATE, for the reason CREATEA gives at
*     the matching loop: CPROC's two LOCATE precedents each search a structure
*     built the other way up from what they appear to search, and a membership
*     test that silently matched nothing would omit nothing here and look
*     exactly like a filter that had worked.
      if copy.it and omit.count > 1 then
         for omit.i = 2 to omit.count       ;* field 1 is the description
*           18 Aug 26 Windows port - AND SO IS THIS ONE, and it is the one that
*           matters: the list holds verb ids and id is a verb id, so if either
*           side is lower-cased without the other NOTHING is omitted and a
*           standard account silently gets the full VOC.  5.12 (b).
            if upcase(omit.rec<omit.i>) = upcase(id) then
               copy.it = @false
               omit.i = omit.count          ;* stop looking
            end
         next omit.i
      end

* 17 Aug 26 Windows port - CONTINUE, and it is demonstrated 30 lines below in
*   this same loop, where a declined type change skips the write.  CREATEA's
*   matching loop says CONTINUE's behaviour inside LOOP ... REPEAT "is not
*   demonstrated anywhere here" and uses a flag round the write instead - that
*   note was wrong about this file, and is left standing there because the flag
*   it chose is not worth a second uncompiled change to correct.
      if not(copy.it) then continue

      read rec from sdsys.file,id then
         new.type = upcase(rec[1,1])
         if new.type = 'P' then new.type = upcase(rec[1,2])

* 17 Aug 26 Windows port - old.rec IS CLEARED FIRST, and it never was.  A failed
*   READ leaves its target variable untouched (op_dio3.c:1254 takes the ELSE
*   clause without writing str_descr), so for a record the account's VOC does not
*   yet hold, old.rec still held the PREVIOUS id's record - and the compare()
*   below decides on that whether to write.
*
*   USUALLY HARMLESS AND OCCASIONALLY NOT: when two adjacent ids normalise to
*   byte-identical records the missing one is silently skipped.  NEWVOC has
*   several pairs that do - CATALOG and CATALOGUE are both V|CA|$CATALOG, CD and
*   COMPILE.DICT are both V|CA|$CD - and a directory select on NTFS comes back in
*   name order, so they arrive adjacent.  An account holding CATALOG and lacking
*   CATALOGUE never got CATALOGUE.
*
*   UPSTREAM TOO - ../sdb64 sd64/sdsys/GPL.BP/LOGIN has no initialisation either.
*   See UPSTREAM_FIXES.md.
         old.rec = ''
         readu old.rec from update.voc.f, id then
            old.type = upcase(old.rec[1,1])
            if old.type = 'P' then old.type = upcase(old.rec[1,2])

            * Special case to avoid prompt for 2.4-15 change to MD item

* 19 Aug 26 Windows port - UPCASE THE ID, PROJECT_STATUS.md 5.12 (b).  id comes
*   from a READNEXT over the update VOC, whose record is 'md' from this release,
*   so an exact comparison against 'MD' stopped matching and every account being
*   updated would be asked about a type change it should never see.
            if upcase(id) = 'MD' and (old.type = 'Q' or old.type = 'F') then
               goto accept.without.query
            end

            if new.type # old.type then
               if yn # 'A' then
                  display
                  display sysmsg(5048, id, old.type) ;* Existing VOC record %1 is of type %2
                  display sysmsg(5049, new.type) :  ;* Update VOC record is of type %1. Replace record(Y/N)?
                  loop
                     input yn
                     yn = upcase(yn)
                  until yn = 'Y' or yn = 'N' or yn = 'A'
                    display sysmsg(5058) : ;* Replace record (Yes/No/All)?
                  repeat
                  if yn = 'N' then
                     release update.voc.f, id
                     continue
                  end
               end
            end
         end

accept.without.query:
         rec<1> = new.type   ;* Remove comment text
         if new.type = 'K' and rec<3> # '' then   ;* Keyword with other use
            s = upcase(rec<3>)
            rec<3> = if s[1,1] = 'P' then s<1>[1,2] else s[1,1]
         end

         if compare(old.rec, rec) then
            write rec to update.voc.f, id
            display '.' :
         end else
            release update.voc.f, id
         end
      end
   repeat
   display 

   close sdsys.file

   return

*
* ======================================================================
* GET.ACC.TIER  -  Read an account's VOC tier from the ACCOUNTS register
*
* 17 Aug 26 Windows port - PROJECT_STATUS.md section 8.  Takes tier.acc.name and
*   sets update.voc.tier for the routine above.  A missing register, a missing
*   record or a record written before tiers existed all give '', which
*   update.voc reads as the full VOC - see SYSCOM/KEYS.H.
*
*   ITS OWN FILE VARIABLE, deliberately.  update.voc is called from inside a loop
*   that has ACCOUNTS open on acc.f with a select list active on channel 12, and
*   reusing either would end that walk part way through with no error and no sign
*   of it.  That call site does not come through here - it has the record already
*   - but the next one might.

get.acc.tier:
   update.voc.tier = ''

   openpath @sdsys:@ds:'accounts' to tier.acc.f then
      read tier.acc.rec from tier.acc.f, upcase(tier.acc.name) then
         update.voc.tier = tier.acc.rec<ACC$TIER>

*        27 Aug 26 Windows port - SUSPENDED IS NOT A VOC TIER, so resolve it to
*        the tier it displaced.  The same substitution as the walk above, and
*        it is here as well as there because these are two independent call
*        sites and neither can see the other's.  A suspended account cannot
*        reach LOGIN at all, but UPDATE.ACCOUNT can be run in an account that
*        was suspended and un-suspended since, and the walk above reaches every
*        account whatever its state.
         if upcase(update.voc.tier) = 'SUSPENDED' then
            update.voc.tier = tier.acc.rec<ACC$PRIOR.TIER>
         end
      end
      close tier.acc.f
   end

   return
end

